{"cid:6440557":{"key":"cid:6440557","title":"lambda-Cyhalothrin","compound":{"key":"cid:6440557","cid":6440557,"qid":"Q27119816","title":"lambda-Cyhalothrin","iupac":"trans-[(R)-cyano-(3-phenoxyphenyl)methyl] (1S,3S)-3-[(Z)-2-chloro-3,3,3-trifluoroprop-1-enyl]-2,2-dimethylcyclopropane-1-carboxylate","wiki_url":null,"cas":"76703-65-6","iuphar":null,"drugbank":null,"chemspider":"4944815","unii":"Z44TGV333K; 375WQ7596S","kegg":null,"chebi":"39327","chembl":"CHEMBL2251732","pdb_ligand":null,"comptox":"DTXSID801033434","echa":null,"description":"chemical compound","kind":null,"atc":null,"mesh":null,"uniprot":null,"ncbi_taxon":null,"gbif":null,"powo":null,"extra_at":"2026-11-08T19:46:28+00:00","eml":null,"smiles":"CC1([C@@H]([C@@H]1C(=O)O[C@@H](C#N)C2=CC(=CC=C2)OC3=CC=CC=C3)/C=C(/C(F)(F)F)\\Cl)C","chembl_phase":0.0,"first_approval":null,"bbw":0,"fda":0,"ema":0,"control":null,"reg_at":"2026-11-10T10:42:53+00:00"},"status":null,"pharm":[{"section":"MeSH Pharmacological Classification","items":[{"section":"MeSH Pharmacological Classification","text":"Chemicals that kill or inhibit the growth of fungi in agricultural applications, on wood, plastics, or other materials, in swimming pools, etc.","source":"Medical Subject Headings (MeSH)","url":"https://id.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/M0008893.html","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/copyright.html"},{"section":"MeSH Pharmacological Classification","text":"Pesticides designed to control insects that are harmful to man. The insects may be directly harmful, as those acting as disease vectors, or indirectly harmful, as destroyers of crops, food products, or textile fabrics.","source":"Medical Subject Headings (MeSH)","url":"https://id.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/M0011389.html","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/copyright.html"}]},{"section":"Metabolism/Metabolites","items":[{"section":"Metabolism/Metabolites","text":"Cyhalothrin has been shown to be well absorbed after oral administration, extensively metabolized, and eliminated as polar conjugates in urine.  The main route of metabolism is, as anticipated, via hydrolysis of the ester linkage. The cyclopropane-carboxylic acid moiety is subsequently excreted via the urine as  the glucuronide conjugate. The 3-phenoxybenzyl moiety is further metabolized by loss of the nitrile group, oxidation of the aldehyde form to a carboxylic acid, aromatic hydroxylation at the 4' position, and formation of the 4- O-sulfate conjugate of 3-(4-hydroxyphenoxy)benzoic acid. (L871)","source":"Toxin and Toxin Target Database (T3DB)","url":"http://www.t3db.ca/toxins/T3D1032","license_url":"https://t3db.ca/downloads"}]}],"safety":[{"section":"Toxicity Summary","items":[{"section":"Toxicity Summary","text":"Both type I and type II pyrethroids exert their effect by prolonging the open phase of the sodium channel gates when a nerve cell is excited. They appear to bind to the membrane lipid phase in the immediate vicinity of the sodium channel, thus modifying the channel kinetics. This blocks the closing of the sodium gates in the nerves, and thus prolongs the return of the membrane potential to its resting state. The repetitive (sensory, motor) neuronal discharge and a prolonged negative afterpotential produces effects quite similar to those produced by DDT, leading to hyperactivity of the nervous system which can result in paralysis and/or death. Other mechanisms of action of pyrethroids include antagonism of gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA)-mediated inhibition, modulation of nicotinic cholinergic transmission, enhancement of noradrenaline release, and actions on calcium ions. They also inhibit calium channels and Ca2+, Mg2+-ATPase. (T10, T18, L857)","source":"Toxin and Toxin Target Database (T3DB)","url":"http://www.t3db.ca/toxins/T3D1032"}]}],"manufacturing":[],"structure":true,"populations":{"child":{"label":"Suitable for children","count":2,"forms":[["Emulsifiable Granules",2]]},"noswallow":{"label":"No tablet or capsule to swallow","count":2,"forms":[["Emulsifiable Granules",2]]}}