ers may act through activation of T-CELLS or by inhibiting the activation of HELPER CELLS. While immunosuppression has been brought about in the past primarily to prevent rejection of transplanted organs, new applications involving mediation of the effects of INTERLEUKINS and other CYTOKINES are emerging.","source":"Medical Subject Headings (MeSH)","url":"https://id.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/M0011171.html","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/copyright.html"}]}],"safety":[],"manufacturing":[{"section":"General Manufacturing Information","items":[{"section":"General Manufacturing Information","text":"Pregna-1,4-diene-3,20-dione, 9-fluoro-11,21-dihydroxy-16,17-[(1-methylethylidene)bis(oxy)]-, (11.beta.,16.alpha.)-: INACTIVE","source":"EPA Chemicals under the TSCA","url":"https://www.epa.gov/chemicals-under-tsca"}]}],"structure":true,"populations":{"child":{"label":"Suitable for children","count":6,"forms":[["Suspension for IM or Intra articular injection",3],["Suspension in ampoule for intramuscular and intra articular injection",1],["Nasal Spray,Suspension",1],["Aqueous suspension for Intramuscular or intra-articular Injection",1]]},"noswallow":{"label":"No tablet or capsule to swallow","count":8,"forms":[["Suspension for IM or Intra articular injection",3],["Metered Nasal Spray",2],["Suspension in ampoule for intramuscular and intra articular injection",1],["Nasal Spray,Suspension",1],["Aqueous suspension for Intramuscular or intra-articular Injection",1]]}},"reg":{"fda":1,"ema":1,"phase":4.0,"first_approval":1960,"bbw":false,"control":null,"withdrawn":[],"reg_checked":true,"aware":null},"chem":{"props":{"key":"cid:6436","title":"Triamcinolone acetonide","products":20,"mw":434.5,"logp":2.42,"tpsa":93.1,"hbd":2,"hba":6,"rotb":2,"rings":5,"heavy":31,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C24H31FO6"},"similar":[{"key":"cid:6215","title":"Fluocinolone acetonide","products":6,"mw":452.5,"logp":2.37,"tpsa":93.1,"hbd":2,"hba":6,"rotb":2,"rings":5,"heavy":32,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C24H30F2O6","similarity":0.735},{"key":"cid:443958","title":"Amcinonide","products":1,"mw":502.6,"logp":3.52,"tpsa":99.1,"hbd":1,"hba":7,"rotb":3,"rings":6,"heavy":36,"lipinski":1,"formula":"C28H35FO7","similarity":0.703},{"key":"cid:31307","title":"Triamcinolone","products":10,"mw":394.4,"logp":0.62,"tpsa":115.1,"hbd":4,"hba":6,"rotb":2,"rings":4,"heavy":28,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C21H27FO6","similarity":0.682},{"key":"cid:9782","title":"Betamethasone","products":73,"mw":392.5,"logp":1.9,"tpsa":94.8,"hbd":3,"hba":5,"rotb":2,"rings":4,"heavy":28,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C22H29FO5","similarity":0.662},{"key":"cid:5743","title":"Dexamethasone","products":115,"mw":392.5,"logp":1.9,"tpsa":94.8,"hbd":3,"hba":5,"rotb":2,"rings":4,"heavy":28,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C22H29FO5","similarity":0.662},{"key":"cid:5311066","title":"Desonide","products":7,"mw":416.5,"logp":2.33,"tpsa":93.1,"hbd":2,"hba":6,"rotb":2,"rings":5,"heavy":30,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C24H32O6","similarity":0.625},{"key":"cid:9642","title":"Fluocinonide","products":4,"mw":494.5,"logp":2.94,"tpsa":99.1,"hbd":1,"hba":7,"rotb":3,"rings":5,"heavy":35,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C26H32F2O7","similarity":0.597},{"key":"cid:5311051","title":"Clobetasol","products":5,"mw":410.9,"logp":3.14,"tpsa":74.6,"hbd":2,"hba":4,"rotb":2,"rings":4,"heavy":28,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C22H28ClFO4","similarity":0.583}]},"eml":false,"names":[["TRIAMCINOLONE ACETONIDE",20]],"atc":[],"by_type":{"human":20},"co_ingredients":[{"key":"cid:6433272","title":"Nystatin [USP:INN:BAN:JAN]","count":9},{"key":"cid:16130140","title":"Gramicidina","count":8},{"key":"cid:8378","title":"Neomycin","count":8},{"key":"cid:338","title":"Salicylic acid","count":2},{"key":"cid:3467","title":"Gentamicin","count":1},{"key":"cid:3676","title":"Lidocaine","count":1},{"key":"qid:Q72500089","title":"Pentosan polysulfate sodium","count":1}]},"cid:5381":{"key":"cid:5381","title":"Tazarotene","compound":{"key":"cid:5381","cid":5381,"qid":"Q3981685","title":"Tazarotene","iupac":"ethyl 6-[2-(4,4-dimethyl-2,3-dihydrothiochromen-6-yl)ethynyl]pyridine-3-carboxylate","wiki_url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tazarotene","cas":"118292-40-3","iuphar":"6952","drugbank":"DB00799","chemspider":"5188","unii":"81BDR9Y8PS","kegg":"C12531; D01132","chebi":"32184","chembl":"CHEMBL1657","pdb_ligand":null,"comptox":"DTXSID5046691","echa":"100.115.380","description":"chemical compound","kind":null,"atc":"D05AX05","mesh":"C086827","uniprot":null,"ncbi_taxon":null,"gbif":null,"powo":null,"extra_at":"2026-11-08T19:46:28+00:00","eml":null,"smiles":"CCOC(=O)C1=CN=C(C=C1)C#CC2=CC3=C(C=C2)SCCC3(C)C","chembl_phase":4.0,"first_approval":1997,"bbw":0,"fda":1,"ema":1,"control":null,"reg_at":"2026-11-10T10:43:32+00:00"},"status":null,"pharm":[{"section":"Drug Indication","items":[{"section":"Drug Indication","text":"Used to treat psoriasis, acne and sun damaged skin (photodamage).","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00799","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Drug Indication","text":"FDA Label","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00799","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"MeSH Pharmacological Classification","items":[{"section":"MeSH Pharmacological Classification","text":"Drugs used to treat or prevent skin disorders or for the routine care of skin.","source":"Medical Subject Headings (MeSH)","url":"https://id.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/M0006057.html","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/copyright.html"},{"section":"MeSH Pharmacological Classification","text":"Agents that soften, separate, and cause desquamation of the cornified epithelium or horny layer of skin. They are used to expose mycelia of infecting fungi or to treat corns, warts, and certain other skin diseases.","source":"Medical Subject Headings (MeSH)","url":"https://id.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/M0011969.html","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/copyright.html"},{"section":"MeSH Pharmacological Classification","text":"An agent that causes the production of physical defects in the developing embryo.","source":"Medical Subject Headings (MeSH)","url":"https://id.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/M0021173.html","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/copyright.html"}]},{"section":"Drug Classes","items":[{"section":"Drug Classes","text":"Breast Feeding; Lactation; Milk, Human; Dermatologic Agents; Keratolytic Agents","source":"Drugs and Lactation Database (LactMed)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/lactmed/LM1101/","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/copyright.html"}]},{"section":"Pharmacodynamics","items":[{"section":"Pharmacodynamics","text":"Following topical application, tazarotene undergoes esterase hydrolysis to form its active metabolite, tazarotenic acid. When treating acne tazarotene may be taken in conjunction with an oral antibiotic. Tazarotene has been shown in peer-reviewed double blinded studies to reduce: mottling and hyperpigmentation, sallowness, fine wrinkling and coarse wrinkling in sun damaged skin. Histological studies have shown that long term (greater than 1 year) use of Tazarotene is associated with a significant reduction in atypical melanocytes and keratocytes - cells considered to be precursors of skin cancer. Some studies have shown long term use of Tazarotene to be associated with increased collagen production and better organization of skin collagen bundles.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00799","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Mechanism of Action","items":[{"section":"Mechanism of Action","text":"Although the exact mechanism of tazarotene action is not known, studies have shown that the active form of the drug (tazarotenic acid) binds to all three members of the retinoic acid receptor (RAR) family: RARa, RARb, and RARg, but shows relative selectivity for RARb, and RARg and may modify gene expression. It also has affinity for RXR receptors.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00799","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","items":[{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"Minimal systemic absorption of tazarotene occurs due to its rapid metabolism in the skin to the active metabolite, tazarotenic acid, which can be systemically absorbed and further metabolized. Gender had no influence on the systemic bioavailability of tazarotenic acid.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00799","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"Tazarotene and tazarotenic acid were metabolized to sulfoxides, sulfones and other polar metabolites which were eliminated through urinary and fecal pathways.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00799","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Protein Binding","items":[{"section":"Protein Binding","text":"The active form of the drug, tazarotenic acid, is highly bound to plasma proteins (>99%).","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00799","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Metabolism/Metabolites","items":[{"section":"Metabolism/Metabolites","text":"Undergoes esterase hydrolysis in skin to form its active metabolite, tazarotenic acid. Tazarotenic acid is further metabolized in skin and, after systemic absorption, hepatically metabolized to sulfoxides, sulfones, and other polar products for elimination.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00799","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Biological Half-Life","items":[{"section":"Biological Half-Life","text":"The half-life of the active form of the drug, tazarotenic acid, is approximately 18 hours in normal and psoriatic patients.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00799","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]}],"safety":[{"section":"Effects During Pregnancy and Lactation","items":[{"section":"Effects During Pregnancy and Lactation","text":"◉ Summary of Use during Lactation Topical tazarotene has not been studied during breastfeeding. Some experts feel it should not be used on a large surface area (perhaps greater than 20% of body surface area) while nursing because of possible absorption. Others recommend that it not be used during breastfeeding because of its suspected mutagenic properties. If tazarotene is used, ensure that the infant's skin does not come into direct contact with the areas of maternal skin that have been treated and the infant does not ingest the product from the mother's skin. ◉ Effects in Breastfed Infants Relevant published information was not found as of the revision date. ◉ Effects on Lactation and Breastmilk Relevant published information was not found as of the revision date.","source":"Drugs and Lactation Database (LactMed)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/lactmed/LM1101/"}]}],"manufacturing":[],"structure":true,"populations":{},"reg":{"fda":1,"ema":1,"phase":4.0,"first_approval":1997,"bbw":false,"control":null,"withdrawn":[],"reg_checked":true,"aware":null},"chem":{"props":{"key":"cid:5381","title":"Tazarotene","products":12,"mw":351.5,"logp":4.43,"tpsa":39.2,"hbd":0,"hba":4,"rotb":2,"rings":3,"heavy":25,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C21H21NO2S"},"similar":[{"key":"cid:2337","title":"Benzocaine","products":8,"mw":165.2,"logp":1.45,"tpsa":52.3,"hbd":1,"hba":3,"rotb":2,"rings":1,"heavy":12,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C9H11NO2","similarity":0.31},{"key":"cid:60150535","title":"Finerenone","products":4,"mw":378.4,"logp":2.99,"tpsa":110.3,"hbd":2,"hba":6,"rotb":5,"rings":3,"heavy":28,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C21H22N4O3","similarity":0.253},{"key":"cid:8365","title":"Ethyl salicylate","products":1,"mw":166.2,"logp":1.57,"tpsa":46.5,"hbd":1,"hba":3,"rotb":2,"rings":1,"heavy":12,"lipin